"""Scoped development of the existing repair/replacement decision owner."""
import re
import expand_hold_period as publisher

SLUG='appliances-repair-replace-short-term-rental'
BODY='''<p class="lead">Before buying a furnished STR, compare appliance repair and replacement using the same required outcome, complete quotes and payment dates. A small repair invoice is not automatically the cheaper acquisition choice; a new unit is not automatically necessary because an older one appears dated. Establish a supported repair scope, a complete installed alternative and a repeat-work sensitivity before committing purchase cash.</p>
<p><a href="/apply/">Bring the repair-versus-replacement comparison to a BNB Accelerator acquisition call</a>. We help integrate supported property findings within our contracted acquisition scope; qualified service professionals determine physical conclusions and counsel determines transaction options.</p>
<h2>Define the decision unit before comparing prices</h2>
<p>Compare one identified appliance or clearly defined group, not an unspecified package. Preserve model and serial identifiers, dated observations, service history and the equipment expected to convey. Ask the qualified reviewer what the proposed repair would correct, what remains unresolved and what evidence supports that conclusion. A seller's description of a minor problem does not establish its cause or price.</p>
<p>Record excluded tests and unresolved safety or installation questions using the <a href="/blog/appliances-inspection-short-term-rental/">appliance inspection register</a>. Do not assign remaining life from a working demonstration or treat age alone as an instruction to replace. This page starts after facts and limitations are documented; it does not diagnose an error code or provide DIY gas, electrical or heavy-equipment instructions.</p>
<p>Define the required opening outcome and acceptable limitations with the property team. A technically repairable unit may still lack a confirmed part or service date. A replacement may require access or qualified connection work absent from the advertised equipment price. Conversely, cosmetic mismatch alone does not demonstrate that a functional unit must be discarded. Keep optional appearance upgrades separate from supported corrective work.</p>
<h2>Put both quotes on a comparable scope register</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Repair evidence</th><th>Replacement evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Exact outcome</td><td>Observed issue, proposed correction and remaining limitations</td><td>Identified substitute and required functions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Complete cash scope</td><td>Parts, labor, visits and applicable fees; identify exclusions</td><td>Unit, delivery, access, removal, applicable taxes and qualified installation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Readiness date</td><td>Part availability, appointment and completion evidence</td><td>Stock confirmation, delivery slot and accepted installation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Support after work</td><td>Actual workmanship/parts terms and who receives a fault report</td><td>Actual warranty terms, local service access and responsible provider</td></tr>
<tr><td>Later uncertainty</td><td>Unresolved condition and a separately stated repeat-work case</td><td>Unresolved installation or support costs; no guaranteed trouble-free life</td></tr>
<tr><td>Buyer payment</td><td>Visit charge, deposit and balance dates</td><td>Order deposit and balance within the complete bid</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Ask whether a diagnosis fee is additional or credited within accepted work. Count it once under the actual terms. If a repair quote already includes a part and its installation, do not add them again. If the replacement bid excludes disposal or connection work, obtain the missing scope before treating it as complete. Dates should distinguish a requested appointment, confirmed slot, completed work and accepted readiness.</p>
<p>Keep recurring utility and service assumptions in the <a href="/blog/appliances-budget-short-term-rental/">dated appliance budget</a>. This comparison isolates decision differences; it should feed that schedule rather than recreate all property expenses. A vendor availability estimate is not a promise of an opening date or future revenue.</p>
<h2>Evaluate coverage separately from the equipment choice</h2>
<p>The <a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/extended-warranties-and-service-contracts">FTC's extended-warranty and service-contract guidance</a> distinguishes paid contracts from warranties that may accompany a product. It recommends checking initial and service fees, reimbursement limits, transfer costs, covered work and claims procedures; waiting for reimbursement can reduce coverage's practical value. The guidance is dated March 2023.</p>
<p>For this acquisition, request the actual documents and written confirmation of buyer eligibility, permitted rental use, transfer requirements and effective coverage. Do not assume consumer guidance establishes rights for every STR transaction or that coverage automatically follows the property. Ask the provider and, where needed, counsel about the actual terms. A paid contract and a manufacturer warranty may cover overlapping work; paying twice does not necessarily buy twice the benefit.</p>
<p>Separate the repair authorization from a potential later claim. Record what must be approved before work, who pays the provider and when any accepted reimbursement would arrive. Do not put an uncertain payment into cash available for a deposit. A warranty term is not an equipment lifespan, and a coverage decision is not an inspection or safety certification.</p>
<p>Check identified units against <a href="https://www.cpsc.gov/Recalls">CPSC recalls and product safety warnings</a> and applicable manufacturer instructions. CPSC notes that remedy availability can change. No match is not proof of safety, and a potential remedy is not money received. Keep stop-use instructions and required qualified follow-up separate from negotiating the cheapest bid.</p>
<h2>Worked comparison: base cost and repeat-work sensitivity</h2>
<p>All figures are invented educational arithmetic, not actual quotes, client results, failure probabilities or a recommended appliance life. Assume the same required outcome is achievable under either documented scope. A repair costs $800. The alternative costs $2,800 fully installed plus $300 of distinct additional opening-delay bills, or $3,100. These figures omit common recurring costs, revenue, tax effects and residual value; they are not total ownership cost or investment return.</p>
<p>The repair base case is $2,300 less. Now test a separate case in which the repaired unit requires a distinct $1,100 second correction within the chosen comparison period and causes $1,500 of distinct additional disruption bills. Repair spending becomes $800 + $1,100 + $1,500 = $3,400, which is $300 more than the $3,100 replacement case. This is a sensitivity, not a prediction that the unit will fail or that replacement cannot fail.</p>
<p>The $2,300 base difference is the maximum additional repair-path cash in this simplified comparison before costs tie. It is not a universal replace-at-a-percentage rule. The stressed extra $2,600 exceeds it by $300. If actual evidence changes either path, change the comparison: a second repair that duplicates the initial scope, a refund, or different installation and delay bills cannot be mechanically added without reconciliation.</p>
<p>Obtain a qualified view of the unresolved condition, parts access and supported alternatives. Do not fabricate a probability to turn this sensitivity into expected cost. Choosing a twenty-four-month comparison window, for example, does not establish that either unit lasts twenty-four months. Apply the buyer's own tolerance for interruption and cash risk alongside the evidence.</p>
<h2>Translate the choice into the acquisition cash plan</h2>
<p>Continue the invented example with $300,000 of accessible designated funds and a $280,000 acquisition allocation that already includes $1,000 for appliance work and a separate $40,000 retained property balance. Stipulate a common $200 diagnosis charge not previously included. The retained balance is not another expense and cannot be silently reused for a separate obligation.</p>
<p>The repair base replaces the $1,000 allowance with $800 and adds the $200 diagnosis: the allocation remains $280,000, leaving $20,000 outside it. The replacement path substitutes $3,100 and adds the same $200: $280,000 − $1,000 + $3,100 + $200 = $282,300, leaving $17,700. The repeat-repair sensitivity adds its distinct $2,600 to the repair base, making $282,600 and leaving $17,400.</p>
<p>If this hypothetical buyer requires $18,000 outside the acquisition for another documented commitment, base repair exceeds that requirement by $2,000, replacement misses it by $300 and stressed repair misses it by $600. That is a buyer-specific constraint, not a standard reserve or loan rule. Confirm deposits and invoices are funded on their actual dates; later booking receipts or hoped-for tax benefits are not today's money.</p>
<p>Disruption figures here are distinct additional cash bills, not gross bookings subtracted again after lowering revenue. A seller credit is not assumed. Use the <a href="/blog/appliances-buying-short-term-rental/">equipment acquisition and seller-treatment guide</a> for actual contract identity, accepted work and disclosed closing treatment. It handles a different decision from this comparison.</p>
<h2>Choose a supported path, then close the outstanding evidence</h2>
<p>Proceed when required work has an acceptable documented scope, qualified readiness evidence, supported dates and funded downside. Reprice a still-viable purchase when complete quotes change its cash needs. Seek valid transaction protection while a decisive answer is outstanding, or decline when the plan depends on unsafe equipment, unsupported coverage or inaccessible funds. An unanswered vendor request does not automatically extend a contract deadline.</p>
<p><a href="/apply/">Compare the shortlisted STR with both complete work scopes and the cash sensitivity</a>. Use the <a href="/blog/buy-str-high-income-large-tax-bill/">high-income buyer roadmap</a> for the broader acquisition. Preserve itemized invoices and condition records for the independent CPA using the <a href="/blog/str-repair-vs-improvement-records/">repair-versus-improvement records checklist</a>; a vendor's repair label does not determine tax treatment. Keep seller and financing records in agreed secure channels.</p>
<p class="small">FTC and CPSC primary guidance reviewed October 7, 2026. Educational only, not diagnosis, installation, safety certification, legal, lending, tax or personalized investment advice. All worked numbers are hypothetical. No equipment life, repair result, warranty payment, opening date, tax benefit or investment return is guaranteed.</p>'''
FAQ=[
('Should an STR buyer always replace an older appliance?', 'No. Compare documented condition, complete corrective and installed scopes, actual support and funded downside. Age or appearance alone does not establish remaining life or a required replacement.'),
('How can I compare appliance repair and replacement fairly?', 'Define the same required outcome and reconcile complete bids, exclusions, diagnosis fees, payment dates and readiness evidence. Keep common recurring costs separate and count each invoice once.'),
('Is a repeat-repair sensitivity a forecast?', 'No. State the additional work and disruption bills as hypothetical inputs without inventing failure probabilities. Reconcile whether later work duplicates or changes either alternative.'),
('Can warranty coverage fund the appliance purchase plan?', 'Do not assume it. Confirm actual buyer and rental-use eligibility, terms and claims timing; uncertain reimbursement is not accessible cash for a deposit or invoice.')]
if __name__=='__main__':
 source=(publisher.ROOT/'blog'/SLUG/'index.html').read_text();publisher.SLUG=SLUG
 publisher.TITLE=re.search(r'<title>(.*?)</title>',source)[1];publisher.H1=re.search(r'<h1>(.*?)</h1>',source)[1]
 publisher.DESC='Before buying an STR, compare complete appliance repair and replacement quotes, warranty limits and repeat-work cash risk with a worked decision worksheet.'
 publisher.BODY=BODY;publisher.FAQ=FAQ
 publisher.main(expected_publication='2026-09-22',hub_attribute='data-appliance-repair-link',hub_paragraph='Compare <a href="/blog/appliances-repair-replace-short-term-rental/">complete appliance repair and replacement scopes</a> with a repeat-work sensitivity before committing the acquisition cash.')
 path=publisher.ROOT/'sitemap/index.html';s=path.read_text();s=re.sub(r'(<a href="/blog/'+SLUG+r'/">)[^<]+',lambda m:m[1]+publisher.H1,s);path.write_text(s)
